# Cube with UV coordinates for texturing
# Each face has its own UV mapping showing the full texture
#
# NOTE: the corners are deliberately split -- 24 vertices rather than 8 -- because
# giving every face the whole texture requires per-face UVs, which shared corners
# cannot express. The consequence is that this is NOT a closed cube: it is six
# disconnected unit squares (V - E + F = 24 - 30 + 12 = 6, six components, all 24
# vertices on a boundary, six boundary loops).
#
# So it is the right mesh for texturing and UV work, and the wrong one for anything
# topological. Decimation, for instance, correctly refuses it: its only interior
# edges are the quad diagonals, and collapsing those deletes whole patches. Use
# cube-closed.obj when you want an actual closed cube.
mtllib cube.mtl

# Front face vertices (z = 0.5)
v -0.5 -0.5  0.5
v  0.5 -0.5  0.5
v  0.5  0.5  0.5
v -0.5  0.5  0.5

# Back face vertices (z = -0.5)
v  0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v -0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v -0.5  0.5 -0.5
v  0.5  0.5 -0.5

# Top face vertices (y = 0.5)
v -0.5  0.5  0.5
v  0.5  0.5  0.5
v  0.5  0.5 -0.5
v -0.5  0.5 -0.5

# Bottom face vertices (y = -0.5)
v -0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v  0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v  0.5 -0.5  0.5
v -0.5 -0.5  0.5

# Right face vertices (x = 0.5)
v  0.5 -0.5  0.5
v  0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v  0.5  0.5 -0.5
v  0.5  0.5  0.5

# Left face vertices (x = -0.5)
v -0.5 -0.5 -0.5
v -0.5 -0.5  0.5
v -0.5  0.5  0.5
v -0.5  0.5 -0.5

# UV coordinates (V flipped for correct vertical, U normal)
vt 0 1
vt 1 1
vt 1 0
vt 0 0

usemtl material0

# Front face
f 1/1 2/2 3/3
f 1/1 3/3 4/4

# Back face
f 5/1 6/2 7/3
f 5/1 7/3 8/4

# Top face
f 9/1 10/2 11/3
f 9/1 11/3 12/4

# Bottom face
f 13/1 14/2 15/3
f 13/1 15/3 16/4

# Right face
f 17/1 18/2 19/3
f 17/1 19/3 20/4

# Left face
f 21/1 22/2 23/3
f 21/1 23/3 24/4
